From 9b68271a8bb7d3f9fd1761a971f6a71970b2b3b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vita-build <vita@build>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 18:55:18 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Vita: native 3D draws split only where faces really overlap,
 each layer's vertices sent to the GPU once

The overlap test that splits a native 3D layer into draws (Renderer3D::DrawUpto) worked on each
face's bounding box at its whole depth range, so a big floor or wall split every draw that crossed
any part of its box. FaceSpans now takes each triangle on its own: only the 16x16 cells its
outline reaches (each cell, widened by a pixel, against the triangle's edges), at the depths its
plane has inside that cell (1/z is linear on the screen, so the corners of the cell's part of the
triangle's box bound it, kept within the triangle's corners' range and widened by 1/1024 for the
float maths). Faces reaching the near plane or not in view space keep every cell at their whole
range, single-cell faces their box. THPS_R3D_EXACT=0 brings back the old test.

Each 3D draw handed vitaGL client-side arrays, which it copies at every draw (twice for an opaque
draw: colour and depth pass). The layer's vertices now get their texture cache slots all at
LayerBegin (what each draw did in turn; the slow-triangle counts per draw come from a running
count) and go into one buffer of graphics memory (glBufferData, eight buffers in turn), which the
draws read in place; the attributes are bound once a layer and the projection uniform is given
to a program only when it changes. If a texture write frees cache slots during the layer, the
remaining draws get their slots again draw by draw and are drawn from client memory, as before.
THPS_GL_LAYERVBO=0 copies at every draw again. The [gl] line adds the layers drawn from a buffer
and the 3D draws that were not.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
